\subsection{Continuous-integration result}

On the release commit, the Python 3.12 CI job reports 721 passed tests, 5 skipped tests, one expected warning from an adversarial ZIP test, and 82.04\% branch coverage. The configured coverage floor is 80\%. Ruff reports no lint failures. The parallel Python 3.10 job and package build also complete successfully. The build path creates a wheel and source distribution and installs the built wheel in a clean environment before running installed-package command-line smoke tests \citep{shadowseed2026}.

These numbers show that the repository's executable contracts ran successfully under the recorded environment. They measure contract execution in that environment. Statistical confidence about omission quality, usefulness, security under adaptive attack, and human outcomes requires independent evaluation.

\subsection{What this evaluation establishes}

The assurance evidence supports five implementation-level claims. Uncertain candidates have a representable state in which they persist without steering. New Gate-controlled authority decisions in the supported runtime pass through the documented validation engine. Provenance and contradiction state participate directly in authority accounting. Actual influence requires a fresh point-of-use decision tied to the current authority version. The live session also keeps baseline and SSL-assisted paths separately observable.

Semantic atomicity, factual correctness, completeness, usefulness, and Gate-policy optimality remain unevaluated. Contract tests cannot answer those empirical and normative questions.

\section{Security and failure analysis}
\label{sec:security}

\subsection{Detector error}

A detector can invent an omission that is already covered, irrelevant, outside scope, or nonsensical. Atomicity heuristics can split a coherent issue badly or fail to split a compound candidate. Weightless creation limits immediate damage. A weak detector can still fill the store with noise and consume reviewer attention. Rate limits, quality calibration, and stronger spam controls remain open work.

\subsection{Correlated recurrence}

Repeated detection may reflect a real unresolved issue. It may equally reflect a stable detector bias. The exploratory policy deliberately allows recurrence to contribute to promotion under its stated rules, so it suits research settings where investigation is cheap and false promotion is visible. The evidence-backed policy is the more defensible choice where influence should depend on external support. Neither policy can infer source independence from repeated model output.

\subsection{Evidence poisoning}

Provenance makes evidence inspectable while source trust remains a separate problem. PoisonedRAG shows that retrieval stores can be attacked directly \citep{zou2025poisonedrag}. A compromised trusted source can therefore produce well-formed, provenance-carrying evidence that is still false. Deployment in consequential domains would need source authentication, trust policies, conflicting-source handling, and review outside the current artifact.

\subsection{Instruction-like seed text}
